

lib_mysqludf_preg  - PCRE functions for mysql
===============================================


== Configuration ==

Most users should be able to simply type ./configure to run the configuration
script.  If mysql is in an unusual place, try: 

./configure --with-mysql=<path to mysql_config>/mysql_config

If libpcre is in an unusual place, try adding:

--with-pcre-prefix=<path to pcre root>

Version 1.1 changes the way NULLs are handled. To restore the legacy NULL handling:

 --enable-legacy-nulls


Example  (on Ubuntu 20.04 with MySQl 8.0.28)
------------------------------
==  Install Build Libraries ==
`sudo apt install build-essential libpcre3-dev git autoconf libtool libmysqlclient-dev`

== Configure == 
`autoreconf -f -i`
`./configure`
`make`
`sudo make install`
` mysql -u root -p < installdb.sql`


Example  (on Ubuntu 26.04 with MariaDB 11.8)
------------------------------
Ubuntu 26.04 (like Debian 13) no longer ships PCRE1 (libpcre3), which this
library uses. Ondřej Surý's PHP repository still has it. Skip this step if
packages.sury.org is already set up for PHP:
`sudo curl -fsSL https://packages.sury.org/php/apt.gpg -o /etc/apt/keyrings/deb.sury.org-php.gpg`
`printf 'Types: deb\nURIs: https://packages.sury.org/php/\nSuites: resolute\nComponents: main\nSigned-By: /etc/apt/keyrings/deb.sury.org-php.gpg\n' | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/php.sources`
`sudo apt update`

==  Install Build Libraries ==
`sudo apt install build-essential autoconf automake libtool libpcre3-dev libmariadb-dev libmariadb-dev-compat libmariadbd-dev`

A server that only runs the library needs just `sudo apt install libpcre3`.

== Configure ==
Build against the MariaDB server headers (libmariadbd-dev): the client
headers' UDF_ARGS is missing fields the server passes. Set libdir to the
server's plugin directory (`SELECT @@plugin_dir;`), because configure
otherwise picks the client plugin directory that mysql_config reports.
`autoreconf -f -i`
`./configure --libdir=/usr/lib/mysql/plugin --disable-static CPPFLAGS=-I/usr/include/mariadb/server`
`make`
`sudo make install`
`sudo mariadb < installdb.sql`

== Test ==
MariaDB's mysqltest has no --include option, so `make test` fails. Run the
tests with mariadb-test (package mariadb-test) instead. They create a
preg_test database, so use a build machine, or drop it afterwards:
`cd test`
`for t in *.test; do cat create_testdb.sql $t | sudo mariadb-test --result-file=${t%.test}.result >/dev/null && echo "ok $t" || echo "FAIL $t"; done`
`sudo mariadb -e 'DROP DATABASE IF EXISTS preg_test'`

Example  (on macosx with fink)
------------------------------
./configure --with-pcre-prefix=/sw  --with-mysql=/sw/bin/mysql_config

Please use:  ./configure --help to see the other options

Example  (on macosx with MAMP)
------------------------------
Follow the example at the link below to compile Mysql and place the lib files in the correct folders
http://addto.it/Patching-MAMP-2.0.5-to-work-with-Sphinx-2.02

Then configure lib_mysqludf_preg with 
./configure --with-mysql=/Applications/MAMP/Library/bin/mysql_config

== Compile == 
Type make


== Install the library == 
make install

This will install the library in the configured installation directory,
which is defaulted to /usr/local/lib.   For the mysql-5.0 series,
this will need to be in the LD_LIBRARY_PATH of the server.  For the
5.1 series server, this directory needs to be the 'plugin' directory
(and still needs to be in the LD_LIBRARY_PATH) of the server).


== Install the SQL functions == 

make installdb

If you receive an 'access denied' type of an error, try using something like:

    make MYSQL="mysql -p" installdb

to enable the ability to enter a password.


To uninstall the functions, you can use:  make uninstalldb

== Run some tests ==
make test

